WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.660
For this week's guest video,
you're probably going to need subtitles.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:06.520
So if they're not on automatically,
you can turn them on in your device settings.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.840
And you'll need them because
this week's guest is Rikki Poynter,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.120
a deaf vlogger who makes videos
in both English and American Sign Language.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:19.040
She has asked me to apologise for accidentally
fingerspelling in front of her face a couple of times here,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.360
apparently that can be rude.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.360
So, Rikki:
[signs in BSL:] Over to you!

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.020
Hello!

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.720
As you’ve read from the title, 
sign language is not universal.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.800
For many years now,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.540
I’ve seen many comments 
from people on the Internet

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:36.560
thinking that there’s only one 
sign language in the whole world.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.380
But… they’re wrong.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:46.480
There are actually about more than 
300 sign languages in the entire world.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.800
Even all English speaking countries 
don’t share the same sign language.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:01:01.320
For example, the United States speaks English 
and uses American Sign Language.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:08.860
England also speaks English, 
but do they use ASL?

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.740
No, they use BSL.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.340
Why is ASL different from BSL?

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:20.320
ASL is more like or based off LSF 
(French Sign Language)

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.640
as the first sign language teacher in the U.S.,

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.000
L... er --

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:34.940
Laurent Clerc, was from France.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.760
He came to the U.S. to teach and thus ASL was born.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.940
Here are some more examples: 
Australia uses Auslan.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:51.280
Canada does use ASL; 
however, Quebec actually uses LSQ.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.640
Now, here’s an exception.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.980
While sign language isn’t exactly universal,

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:05.520
there is this thing called 
International Sign or IS.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:14.200
This is typically used in big events 
where deaf people from all over gather.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:19.810
For example, Deaflympics.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.810
Oh, hey, I spelled it right.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:26.470
But it’s important to know that IS also differs.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.900
It's also not the same everywhere.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:33.000
Of course, just like you may see 
similarities in spoken languages,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.380
you will see them in various sign languages.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:40.220
For example, the ASL sign for “I’m sorry” is this.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.540
You’ll see the same one used in BSL.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.430
Sometimes, you’ll see a sign 
used in multiple sign languages.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:03:03.490
“Where?” in ASL is “What?” in BSL.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.090
Naturally, people wonder,

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:14.560
“Why can’t we have just one sign language? 
Wouldn’t it be easier that way?”

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:21.500
If that happened, that would make things boring.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.160
Yes, it would probably make things easier, but

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.390
we already have different spoken languages, 
so why not have different sign languages?

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:34.290
Different cultures and languages make life interesting.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.070
It makes us all unique.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.990
When it comes to deaf history,

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:46.720
there’s a lot of detail.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:54.300
But if you want to learn more, 
I definitely encourage you to do so.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:00.160
It can be difficult to keep it condensed for a video.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.420
I hope you all learned something new today.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:03.960
Thank you all for watching and

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.900
thank you, Tom, for allowing me 
to be here to make this video for you.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.460
Maybe I’ll see you all later? Bye!

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.220
Go subscribe to Rikki's channel!
And remember, she's a vlogger:

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.100
that is a channel about her life, not just
"all your sign language questions answered"!

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.520
I figure I should set expectations there.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:25.320
Next week, a video that's a little bit related
but also a little bit musical.

